<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ghilajharighat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1663">1663</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-mughal-conflicts">Ahom–Mughal conflicts</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Ghilajharighat was signed in January 1663 between the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadhwaj-singha">Jayadhwaj Singha</a> and the Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Mir Jumla, after the Mughal occupation of the Ahom capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> ceded western territories including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>, agreed to pay a heavy war indemnity and annual tribute, and sent a princess and hostages to the Mughal court. They recovered Guwahati in 1667.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-asurar-ali">Treaty of Asurar Ali</a> of 1639 fixed the limit between Ahom and Mughal interests at the Barnadi river on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> and the Asurar Ali on the south. During the Mughal war of succession of 1658–1659 Pran Narayan, king of Koch Bihar, attacked the Mughal faujdar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> refused a Koch offer to partition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamrup">Kamrup</a>, drove the Koches out and took the whole valley up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankosh-river">Sankosh River</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jumla-ii">Mir Jumla II</a> subahdar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. He annexed Koch Bihar in December 1661, recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> on 5 February 1662 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> on 17 March. The rains from April turned the country to marsh and brought food shortage and cholera to the camps, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> waged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare devised by Atan Burhagohain. When the rains ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> recovered and the king withdrew to Namrup. The Ahom commander-in-chief, Baduli Phukan, defected at the end of November, and Mir Jumla, by then ill, reached Tipam by 18 December with a worn-out army eager to go home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Atan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhagohain">Burhagohain</a> persuaded the king that further fighting was futile and sent four emissaries offering tribute if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. Mir Jumla, who had refused everything short of unconditional surrender, took the opening and left the bargaining to Dilir Khan. The terms are pieced together from Persian and Assamese sources that do not fully agree.</p><p>The king accepted Mughal vassalage; his daughter Ramani Gabharu went to the imperial harem with a dowry of 20,000 tolas of gold and 120,000 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. The indemnity was 300,000 tolas of silver within a year and 90 elephants in instalments, the annual tribute 20 elephants. Four sons of leading nobles were to be hostages. The ceded land ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> to the Bharali River on the north bank and to the Kolong River on the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The princess left on 5 January 1663 with the gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and part of the elephants, and Mir Jumla ordered the retreat from Tipam on 10 January. He died on board on 31 March 1663. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadhwaj-singha">Jayadhwaj Singha</a> founded a new capital at Bakata and died in November 1663; his cousin succeeded as Chakradhar Singha. Neither king could accept tributary status, and the terms were only partly fulfilled. The treaty was the root of the later conflicts, which ended with the Mughal defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saraighat">Battle of Saraighat</a> in 1671 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-itakhuli">Battle of Itakhuli</a> in 1682, the end of Mughal interests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Ghilajharighat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1662 the Mughal governor of Bengal, Mir Jumla, invaded, occupied the capital Garhgaon and forced Jayadhwaj Singha to accept the harsh <b>Treaty of Ghilajharighat</b> in 1663, but disease and monsoon floods ruined the Mughal army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
